From 6b4e3cd11c4c643ca9bc1d7e35d119c150181dfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:42:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/20] dos: Add an off-by-default HSDir defense against
 unknown previous hops

Tor and Arti clients and onion services build the circuit of each onion
service descriptor fetch or upload through relays from the consensus.
Stock software doesn't break that, apart from brief windows where an
HSDir's consensus lags a client's, but a previous hop missing from the
consensus escapes DoSCircuitCreation, which only counts client
connections (tor#41339).

Add HSDirRefuseUnknownPrevHop, an AUTOBOOL option whose "auto" default
follows the consensus parameter of the same name, which defaults to 0.
When enabled, an HSDir refuses a request whose previous hop is neither
in its consensus nor in its descriptor list, the check
RefuseUnknownExits applies to exit streams. It applies after the limit
of one HSDir request per circuit, and refuses as that limit does, by
closing the circuit without an answer: clients retry at another HSDir,
and a 503 would also make them avoid this relay for other directory
requests. As with DoSRefuseSingleHopClientRendezvous, it only applies on
public relays, and dos_should_refuse_single_hop_client() now shares its
helper.

The requests this defense applies to are already counted on the
MetricsPort, as tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_unknown_prev_hop"}, so
operators can see what enabling it would refuse. Refused requests are
counted as action "refused" in tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_total and
tor_relay_hsdir_upload_total, so each tunneled request is still counted
once. The heartbeat shows the count once the defense is enabled.

Part of #41339.
